# Copy to build.conf and edit. Sourced by build.sh; environment variables of
# the same name override these values.
#
# Hostname: letters/digits/hyphens, max 63 chars.
# Usernames: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]{0,30}$ and must differ; avoid system names
# (bin, daemon, mail, ...). The admin user gets wheel/sudo; the desktop user
# autostarts sway on tty1.

KS_HOSTNAME=laptop
KS_ADMIN_USER=admin
KS_DESKTOP_USER=me

# System timezone, a name under /usr/share/zoneinfo (default: UTC).
#KS_TIMEZONE=Europe/Berlin

# Firmware for THIS machine's hardware, space-separated. Fedora ships
# vendor firmware as packages that linux-firmware at most recommends,
# and this kickstart installs no weak dependency: a device whose
# package is not named here runs without firmware (no WiFi, no GPU
# acceleration, no Bluetooth, no SOF sound, factory CPU microcode).
#   GPU:        amd-gpu-firmware intel-gpu-firmware nvidia-gpu-firmware
#   WiFi/BT:    iwlwifi-mvm-firmware iwlwifi-dvm-firmware (Intel)
#               atheros-firmware (Qualcomm/Atheros) realtek-firmware
#               brcmfmac-firmware (Broadcom) mt7xxx-firmware (MediaTek)
#               tiwilink-firmware nxpwireless-firmware
#   WWAN:       qcom-wwan-firmware
#   Audio:      alsa-sof-firmware (Intel SOF DSP laptops)
#               cirrus-audio-firmware intel-audio-firmware
#   CPU:        microcode_ctl (Intel) amd-ucode-firmware (AMD)
# After the first boot, "journalctl -k | grep -i firmware" names anything
# the kernel asked for and did not find.
#KS_FIRMWARE="microcode_ctl intel-gpu-firmware iwlwifi-mvm-firmware"

# Resolvers of the installed system and of the installer, IPv4, comma and
# space separated; the firewall accepts port 53 to these only (default:
# Cloudflare's "1.1.1.1, 1.0.0.1").
#KS_RESOLVERS="9.9.9.9, 149.112.112.112"

# Firmware boot-entry label (default: "Fedora Linux"); it also names this
# install's Secure Boot signing key in the firmware's key list.
# It is stored in motherboard NVRAM and VISIBLE to anyone at the boot menu
# before any password. The default is deliberately generic, with nothing
# of this machine in it, and safe to reuse identically on every disk:
# booting never depends on the label. Install-time cleanup matches entries
# by GPT partition UUID only (previous installs of the chosen disk +
# anaconda's grub entry); other disks' entries are never touched, attached
# or unplugged.
# A distinct label is needed only to tell entries apart; it stays neutral,
# meaningful to the owner and generic to an observer.
# Allowed: letters, digits, space, ()._-  max 40 chars.
#KS_BOOT_LABEL="Fedora A"
